WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.460
Volleyball, here's your chance to bump, set, and spike your way to victory.

00:00:19.820 --> 00:00:26.820
And welcome to NEStalgia, a chronological exploration of every NES game released in North America.

00:00:27.040 --> 00:00:27.900
I'm Mike.

00:00:28.060 --> 00:00:29.440
I'm Sean. And I'm Joe.

00:00:29.940 --> 00:00:35.620
Guys, today we're talking about volleyball, developed by Nintendo and PAX Softnica.

00:00:36.960 --> 00:00:38.500
Now just Softnica.

00:00:39.120 --> 00:00:43.480
Okay. Not that it mattered, I don't think the PAX had any, like, it wasn't like, oh, now we're

00:00:43.480 --> 00:00:45.500
free of our shackles and we're gonna make good games.

00:00:47.820 --> 00:00:53.740
But yeah, we got volleyball here, another sports game, in many ways feels like a NES launch game.

00:00:53.740 --> 00:01:00.100
Yeah, if it wasn't for that incessant, like, theme music at the beginning of every single one of these, it's killing me.

00:01:00.100 --> 00:01:02.240
What's it been so far? Baseball, soccer, right?

00:01:02.340 --> 00:01:03.440
Baseball, soccer, and tennis.

00:01:03.660 --> 00:01:05.160
And tennis. And I'll tell you a funny story.

00:01:05.160 --> 00:01:18.180
In the, in like the ZX Sharp, which was only in Japan, they like, had a relationship between Hudson and Nintendo to make some of those games for the ZX Sharp as well.

00:01:18.600 --> 00:01:20.320
And it wasn't called the ZX Sharp.

00:01:20.440 --> 00:01:21.860
Don't quote me on that. It's called something stupid.

00:01:21.860 --> 00:01:23.520
But it's a computer for Japan.

00:01:23.860 --> 00:01:28.300
And they made a port of golf for, for this computer system at home.

00:01:28.420 --> 00:01:30.120
And they even put the music in there.

00:01:30.260 --> 00:01:34.760
Oh my God. They put this theme song, like a computerized version of it.

00:01:34.920 --> 00:01:38.740
But it's like, this song is just, I guess like, it's right up there.

00:01:38.840 --> 00:01:40.900
It's like one of Nintendo's sports theme.

00:01:41.380 --> 00:01:43.340
It doesn't sound sporty.

00:01:43.500 --> 00:01:46.460
Yeah, it's the least competitive sounding song I can think of.

00:01:46.460 --> 00:01:48.840
I don't even think it got an homage in Wii Sports.

00:01:49.060 --> 00:01:50.380
Like, I don't, I don't hear it anywhere in there.

00:01:50.680 --> 00:02:00.400
So, but yeah, you definitely know you're playing a first party Nintendo game when you hear that, that startup sound and get two options.

00:02:01.440 --> 00:02:06.920
The two options in this one being training mode and game mode.

00:02:07.920 --> 00:02:10.800
But before, before we do that, why don't we go through first impressions?

00:02:18.600 --> 00:02:23.120
Well, my first impression of this game was I'm really bad at it.

00:02:23.320 --> 00:02:32.420
And the Z-axis is one of those problems again, where I'm just trying to, I know how to bump, set, and spike.

00:02:32.420 --> 00:02:35.160
I played volleyball in gym class.

00:02:35.300 --> 00:02:38.340
Okay. So I know the rules of the road.

00:02:38.740 --> 00:02:39.960
Do you get a certificate for that?

00:02:40.180 --> 00:02:43.320
You get an A as long as you show up.

00:02:43.480 --> 00:02:44.800
Nice. Yeah. Does that have a good word?

00:02:44.920 --> 00:02:47.500
The certificate is the report card.

00:02:47.640 --> 00:02:51.080
Nice. Very good. But anyway, I know the rules of volleyball.

00:02:51.140 --> 00:02:54.460
I know that you have to be under the ball to hit it usually.

00:02:55.080 --> 00:02:57.400
And I was under the ball, and I could never hit it.

00:02:58.260 --> 00:03:02.720
Yeah. I think that's a common problem we're going to run into as we talk into this game.

00:03:02.880 --> 00:03:04.440
But Joe, any first impressions?

00:03:04.920 --> 00:03:06.700
Same thing before I read the manual.

00:03:07.360 --> 00:03:08.720
I mean, same thing after I read the manual, too.

00:03:09.120 --> 00:03:12.360
Before I read the manual. I thought you were going to tell me you got enlightened after the manual.

00:03:12.660 --> 00:03:15.620
I mean, the manual has a lot of information in it.

00:03:15.680 --> 00:03:16.520
There's a lot you can do.

00:03:16.660 --> 00:03:23.080
And I feel like if any of us took a long time to memorize and learn these things, we could probably be good at this game.

00:03:24.440 --> 00:03:27.660
But my first impression is probably this is not a pick-up-and-play game.

00:03:28.040 --> 00:03:31.620
Also, that the volleyball players are all, like, dancing the whole time.

00:03:31.740 --> 00:03:32.960
They're really moving their hips the whole time.

00:03:33.060 --> 00:03:34.080
Yeah, but you weren't talking about dancing before.

00:03:34.460 --> 00:03:36.800
Huh? What are you talking about?

00:03:37.280 --> 00:03:38.420
Dancing. Is it twerking?

00:03:38.580 --> 00:03:40.720
Dancing? Might as well acknowledge that dancing.

00:03:40.860 --> 00:03:42.600
It's like they're humping the air, essentially.

00:03:43.000 --> 00:03:44.580
Yeah, it's a dance. It's just in rhythm, though, too.

00:03:44.760 --> 00:03:45.800
I'm trying to keep it, you know, family-oriented.

00:03:45.800 --> 00:03:48.100
Even your opponents are, like, synced up with you.

00:03:48.300 --> 00:03:49.840
Everybody is just synced up.

00:03:49.980 --> 00:03:51.060
It's a grand old time.

00:03:51.060 --> 00:03:54.780
Yeah, I don't think there's any, I don't remember any music while you're actually playing.

00:03:55.280 --> 00:03:56.700
No, there's music. While you're playing?

00:03:57.340 --> 00:03:58.560
I'm pretty sure. Okay.

00:03:58.680 --> 00:03:59.960
It's terrible. All right, yeah.

00:04:00.040 --> 00:04:01.440
Maybe that's why I just drowned it out.

00:04:01.540 --> 00:04:05.900
But I was going to say that whoever's actually at that gymnasium is just dropping a fat beat.

00:04:06.280 --> 00:04:07.260
You know, like, everybody's...

00:04:07.260 --> 00:04:08.800
You know what? I didn't think about it until you just said it.

00:04:08.800 --> 00:04:09.660
They are synced up.

00:04:09.740 --> 00:04:10.840
That makes it so much funnier.

00:04:10.940 --> 00:04:12.000
Right. Everybody is just...

00:04:12.000 --> 00:04:12.900
They're all, like, perfectly in sync. Yep.

00:04:13.240 --> 00:04:16.580
It's like... Just humping the air. Yeah, it's really more of a dance-off than it is volleyball.

00:04:16.780 --> 00:04:19.120
That's true. It's just a side effect of what's happening.

00:04:20.520 --> 00:04:23.160
All right. My first impressions aren't that much different.

00:04:23.300 --> 00:04:26.940
I really agree with the idea that this is not a pick-up-and-play game.

00:04:27.120 --> 00:04:33.820
I was kind of shocked at how, like, you know, as long as you understand the real-life rules

00:04:33.820 --> 00:04:40.800
of a game like baseball or tennis or golf, as long as you understand those rules, all

00:04:40.800 --> 00:04:44.140
of those games that Nintendo's offered us have been, like, you know, pick-up-and-play

00:04:44.140 --> 00:04:46.500
style games. You don't have to go right to the manual.

00:04:46.920 --> 00:04:54.860
So, I think, for me, it was like, this is just weird that we have volleyball and you

00:04:54.860 --> 00:04:56.560
can't play it, you know, like, right off the bat.

00:04:56.620 --> 00:04:57.660
You have to kind of figure it out.

00:04:58.100 --> 00:05:05.720
Yeah, because another part that we haven't seen yet in the sports games, I think, is the

00:05:05.720 --> 00:05:08.660
idea of controlling more than one character at once.

00:05:09.280 --> 00:05:12.760
And I think that causes a lot more issues.

00:05:12.760 --> 00:05:18.000
And I think just real fast before we go any further into talking about the game, we should

00:05:18.000 --> 00:05:21.300
probably just talk about the objective, because there are, like, different kinds of volleyball

00:05:21.300 --> 00:05:22.820
here. And I think that's important.

00:05:23.040 --> 00:05:26.940
This is a, like, six-on-six indoor volleyball style game.

00:05:27.080 --> 00:05:28.700
This isn't two-on-two beach volleyball.

00:05:28.960 --> 00:05:35.540
Like, I think most people are familiar with the Dead or Alive series, Extreme Beach Volleyball.

00:05:36.360 --> 00:05:37.540
So, yeah, it's not like that.

00:05:37.700 --> 00:05:38.660
It's six-on-six.

00:05:38.860 --> 00:05:40.900
And you can play as men or women.

00:05:41.240 --> 00:05:44.920
And this game does not, like, you know, no different under each one.

00:05:45.100 --> 00:05:48.220
Oh, it's different. And it's a little insulting to the women's game.

00:05:48.220 --> 00:05:50.340
Uh-oh. You picked on something I clearly didn't.

00:05:50.440 --> 00:05:52.400
I only played just the women's game.

00:05:52.400 --> 00:05:53.440
I think I only played the women's.

00:05:53.440 --> 00:05:55.580
Yeah, I don't know why. The women's game is slower.

00:05:56.040 --> 00:05:57.080
Oh, shoot. Yeah.

00:05:57.080 --> 00:06:01.840
Ouch. I was about to say, like, how progressive that was in Nintendo to have, like, no gender

00:06:01.840 --> 00:06:04.460
bias. And then you come in and be like, eh, well.

00:06:04.500 --> 00:06:06.820
Yeah. It's a little bit of a problem.

00:06:06.920 --> 00:06:10.900
I guess now that you say that, it almost looks like women's and men's are, like, mode

00:06:10.900 --> 00:06:12.480
A, mode B in a lot of other games.

00:06:12.620 --> 00:06:14.340
Oh, yeah. So, that is what it's all.

00:06:14.420 --> 00:06:20.260
Yeah, yeah, yeah. So, since we're talking about mode A and mode B, we should probably talk about the training

00:06:20.260 --> 00:06:24.860
mode first. I found that to be helpful after deciding to go to it.

00:06:24.860 --> 00:06:27.880
You know, game is the option that's offered to you right away.

00:06:28.180 --> 00:06:30.420
Yeah. You have to hit select to get over to the training mode.

00:06:30.600 --> 00:06:32.020
But I found that to be helpful.

00:06:32.420 --> 00:06:37.500
Yeah, I thought it was the only mode that I could actually compete.

00:06:38.360 --> 00:06:40.980
I only played the training mode for, like, a minute.

00:06:41.240 --> 00:06:47.040
I did tell you guys off the mic, but I guess I'll just admit it to the world that I actually,

00:06:47.220 --> 00:06:49.200
while playing it, thought I was controlling both sides.

00:06:49.320 --> 00:06:53.600
Like, that's how little idea I had of what was going on in this volleyball game.

00:06:53.600 --> 00:06:58.080
The first time I started up the game, I hit the start button and thought that, like,

00:06:58.100 --> 00:06:59.560
I guess it just didn't register or something.

00:06:59.760 --> 00:07:01.160
And I thought I was in the game.

00:07:01.580 --> 00:07:03.560
And I thought I was playing as the team.

00:07:03.680 --> 00:07:05.100
But it was just a video.

00:07:05.300 --> 00:07:08.960
It was just like, you know, the demo of the, you know, as Joe famously knows, there's a

00:07:08.960 --> 00:07:11.240
guy playing the demo. Yeah, the guy who plays the demo.

00:07:11.300 --> 00:07:12.520
The guy who plays the demo. Live.

00:07:12.860 --> 00:07:13.760
Trapped. When you hit it.

00:07:13.800 --> 00:07:15.040
In the machine. Trapped in the machine.

00:07:15.040 --> 00:07:16.860
I have no mouth, but I must scream.

00:07:18.160 --> 00:07:22.260
But yeah, no, I thought that the training was like, oh, you control both sides, so you

00:07:22.260 --> 00:07:25.380
get an idea of the cadence or something, and then you get into the real game and you just

00:07:25.380 --> 00:07:26.400
pick one side. Yeah.

00:07:26.460 --> 00:07:27.380
No, that wasn't what was happening.

00:07:27.660 --> 00:07:29.060
I don't know if that would have been more helpful, though.

00:07:29.520 --> 00:07:31.720
Like, being able to, like, I think it would have been even more stressful.

00:07:31.720 --> 00:07:33.440
I was confused while I was watching.

00:07:33.520 --> 00:07:37.100
But then you can kind of just, you can know where, if you know where the opponent's going

00:07:37.100 --> 00:07:39.360
to hit it, you know where to move your next character.

00:07:39.460 --> 00:07:43.280
Okay, so what I liked about the training mode was, as Joe mentions, it highlights who you're

00:07:43.280 --> 00:07:47.700
playing as, it changes their, everybody's wearing white shirts, and then it changes them over

00:07:47.700 --> 00:07:49.920
to, like, a pink or something.

00:07:50.180 --> 00:07:55.660
Well, for context, the only reason that this is a thing in training mode is because when

00:07:55.660 --> 00:08:04.060
you're in a regular game, and I actually think I need to take another step backwards, basically

00:08:04.060 --> 00:08:07.100
you only control, like, half of your team at a time.

00:08:07.200 --> 00:08:08.200
You control them in rows.

00:08:08.340 --> 00:08:12.600
Well, in rows, or sometimes in quarters.

00:08:13.280 --> 00:08:15.940
Um, if it goes to, like, one side or the other.

00:08:16.380 --> 00:08:20.300
And in a regular game, there's no designation.

00:08:20.300 --> 00:08:25.360
It does not show you which half of your team you are controlling.

00:08:25.660 --> 00:08:31.820
It, you just have to sort of make a guess that, yes, I'm controlling these three players.

00:08:32.540 --> 00:08:37.820
What training mode does is it actually gives you a tool to know which players you are controlling.

00:08:38.000 --> 00:08:42.220
Yeah. And I think that that should have just been a feature in the regular game.

00:08:42.220 --> 00:08:45.400
In the regular game, too. I don't understand why, like, why that isn't a feature.

00:08:45.520 --> 00:08:51.440
Why wouldn't you? Especially because, just to be clear here, too, other characters move that aren't you.

00:08:51.760 --> 00:08:56.040
Yeah. In the game. Like, and you won't necessarily know which ones you're controlling unless you have a good

00:08:56.040 --> 00:08:57.100
feel for this game.

00:08:57.100 --> 00:09:01.120
I almost think, even though it's not ideal, I almost think for a gameplay, I think they

00:09:01.120 --> 00:09:03.680
would have been better off if all the characters were just tied together.

00:09:03.920 --> 00:09:06.380
Like, and then you could at least see you're controlling all of them.

00:09:06.380 --> 00:09:08.400
To be honest, that was my... But seriously, then you can pass to each other.

00:09:08.560 --> 00:09:12.400
And, like... That was going to be my offered solution because I know it sounds archaic, Sean, but, like,

00:09:12.500 --> 00:09:16.580
that was, like, the 10-yard fight style of things in a weird way where it was, like,

00:09:16.960 --> 00:09:18.200
that's how defense...

00:09:18.200 --> 00:09:21.640
Like, you always controlled one person, but the rest of your team all moved, you know,

00:09:21.760 --> 00:09:24.780
as a group. I just kind of felt like I would agree with that.

00:09:24.880 --> 00:09:29.600
And then you're, like, you're just the server or, like, you know, you hit, like, a different

00:09:29.600 --> 00:09:31.180
button to do it.

00:09:31.360 --> 00:09:34.460
And you can set to yourself, even though it's kind of going to be weird to have to move.

00:09:34.460 --> 00:09:38.360
I think it's something that the Super Nintendo would have done, you know, having the ability

00:09:38.360 --> 00:09:40.720
to have an X and Y button for additional toggles.

00:09:41.200 --> 00:09:43.980
This, obviously, there's just, you know, like, what button would you map it to?

00:09:44.520 --> 00:09:47.680
But, you know, for me, it's like, that would have been the ideal way.

00:09:47.680 --> 00:09:50.820
To only control either a single character or a group of characters.

00:09:50.960 --> 00:09:51.820
It should have been, like...

00:09:51.820 --> 00:09:53.760
And this one makes it more like foosball.

00:09:54.020 --> 00:09:55.960
Yeah. But it's very hard to figure out.

00:09:56.420 --> 00:10:01.180
I still prefer the training mode version to your all or nothing.

00:10:01.560 --> 00:10:08.300
Okay. But just because I think that if you make it so that you only control one or, like, you

00:10:08.300 --> 00:10:12.420
serve and then you set to yourself, like, that seems too simplified to me.

00:10:12.560 --> 00:10:13.460
But, I mean...

00:10:14.140 --> 00:10:15.520
It's tennis. I'm not tennis.

00:10:16.040 --> 00:10:16.900
It's volleyball.

00:10:16.900 --> 00:10:17.800
It's sports.

00:10:18.520 --> 00:10:19.420
It is a sport game.

00:10:20.120 --> 00:10:23.240
So, okay. So, now you've finally figured out which players you control.

00:10:23.500 --> 00:10:25.020
Are you returning the hit?

00:10:25.240 --> 00:10:28.540
No. I'll tell you what, Mike.

00:10:30.520 --> 00:10:34.320
Because there are times when it seems to give you some leeway.

00:10:34.700 --> 00:10:37.740
It seems to say, like, oh, you meant to do this.

00:10:37.900 --> 00:10:39.760
Like, you're obviously in reach of the ball.

00:10:39.760 --> 00:10:41.140
Mm-hmm. And you'll hit the ball.

00:10:41.280 --> 00:10:43.080
Or you might set it to your teammate.

00:10:43.080 --> 00:10:45.280
Or you might just bump it over.

00:10:46.340 --> 00:10:47.920
But other times, it'll just be...

00:10:47.920 --> 00:10:50.220
You'll be standing right under it.

00:10:50.260 --> 00:10:51.400
And you will hit the button.

00:10:51.580 --> 00:10:53.120
And it will just fall to the ground.

00:10:53.140 --> 00:10:56.660
And then that really annoying fake whistle will go.

00:10:56.880 --> 00:10:57.700
The train whistle.

00:10:58.640 --> 00:11:00.680
Right? Yes. No, it's...

00:11:00.680 --> 00:11:01.640
Yes, that's what it sounds like.

00:11:01.660 --> 00:11:02.880
It's clearly not a train.

00:11:03.400 --> 00:11:04.740
Yeah. That's what it sounds like.

00:11:04.860 --> 00:11:06.640
I get that sometimes, like, regardless.

00:11:06.860 --> 00:11:08.700
Like, I feel like I have bumped the ball.

00:11:08.840 --> 00:11:10.360
But the ref is like, no, that's illegal.

00:11:10.880 --> 00:11:13.460
You know? Like, you're not supposed to touch that ball.

00:11:13.560 --> 00:11:15.880
And it's like, that's the only thing you were supposed to do in this game.

00:11:17.800 --> 00:11:18.800
So that... Yeah.

00:11:19.000 --> 00:11:21.000
I think that that's, like, a weird thing.

00:11:21.120 --> 00:11:25.780
Is that, like, after you've figured out getting over the hump of even just, like, which characters do I control?

00:11:26.020 --> 00:11:28.320
Now it's like, well, good luck using them.

00:11:28.720 --> 00:11:31.760
Yeah. So then you sort of...

00:11:31.760 --> 00:11:32.780
Like, once you...

00:11:33.300 --> 00:11:37.480
You're at, like, a 50% success rate of even returning the ball.

00:11:37.720 --> 00:11:39.320
Then you have to figure out how to score.

00:11:39.320 --> 00:11:41.080
And the whole...

00:11:41.080 --> 00:11:51.280
The whole setup of how you go about getting from bump to set to spike is just a, like, a maddening exercise in futility.

00:11:51.480 --> 00:11:53.640
Because I think I've spiked...

00:11:53.640 --> 00:11:56.100
I was able to spike it maybe a half a dozen times.

00:11:56.280 --> 00:11:58.600
And I think twice I scored.

00:11:58.780 --> 00:12:02.520
Okay. I was very good at blocking with the front row.

00:12:02.520 --> 00:12:03.520
I got better with that.

00:12:03.560 --> 00:12:04.660
Yeah. In training mode.

00:12:04.860 --> 00:12:09.820
Yeah. I think, like, you know, it's cool that they have these things going on.

00:12:10.080 --> 00:12:19.960
But even more so than tennis, I remember on the tennis episode we were very critical of that Z-axis and just, like, you know, it was maybe a little harder than it needed to be for a fun game.

00:12:20.160 --> 00:12:24.240
You know? You know, in this one, it's, like, it's nice that they offer these options.

00:12:24.240 --> 00:12:28.060
But at the same time, how much more complicated does it make the game?

00:12:28.060 --> 00:12:32.700
And how much more fun would it have just been if it was, like, auto-lock-on to the ball.

00:12:32.700 --> 00:12:38.040
Right. Yeah. And then figure out your strategy in terms of, like, bumping, setting, and spiking, you know?

00:12:38.200 --> 00:12:39.440
And did you guys read the manual?

00:12:39.680 --> 00:12:47.540
I did. So once you look in there, you see that there's, like, there's tons of, like, weird button combinations to spike it and to do it, you know?

00:12:47.640 --> 00:12:52.280
Yeah. And I think it's a little—I think, honestly, like I said, I kind of stand by what I said at the beginning.

00:12:52.280 --> 00:13:01.220
I think that if we were, for some reason, really interested in continuing playing this game and, like, we put a lot of time into it, we probably could get good at it and, like, enjoy it a lot more.

00:13:01.760 --> 00:13:05.300
But I just don't see a lot of people really wanting to do that.

00:13:05.300 --> 00:13:12.400
But would it be any more rewarding than, like, say, NES baseball or, you know, NES golf where it's, like, it doesn't take much to get started?

00:13:12.720 --> 00:13:14.500
Obviously, the more you play, the better you get.

00:13:14.640 --> 00:13:20.040
But, like, the fact—like, would getting good at this game be any more rewarding than just playing those games normally?

00:13:20.040 --> 00:13:26.660
Yeah, I think that what I'm saying is just, like, if somebody out there just, like, is crazy about volleyball, this would probably be—

00:13:26.660 --> 00:13:33.920
I'm just kind of, like, an opinion that doesn't really come from anywhere, but, like, I think that if you're crazy, crazy about volleyball, you'd probably like this better.

00:13:34.020 --> 00:13:34.840
You're enabling people.

00:13:34.920 --> 00:13:37.240
Someone who's crazy about baseball would like NES baseball.

00:13:37.420 --> 00:13:38.620
Sure, yeah, that's true.

00:13:38.680 --> 00:13:44.720
Because if you really, really want to put, like, your all into this, like, you can probably do more in this than you can do in NES baseball.

00:13:45.240 --> 00:13:46.360
That may be true.

00:13:46.360 --> 00:13:57.020
I think, though, that in a game design perspective, this is, like, this is an example of trying to fit too much onto a very simple controller.

00:13:57.380 --> 00:14:06.180
And that—it's a good idea to try and give people that want the most control the most control.

00:14:06.380 --> 00:14:18.660
Right. By giving them all these options, but it also introduces problems in which, like, I—like, the B button is to softly, like, set it to yourself, more or less.

00:14:18.800 --> 00:14:24.500
Right. The B button, while you're pressing right or left, is to spike the ball.

00:14:24.900 --> 00:14:28.980
So I'll just try and get under the ball and set it to myself.

00:14:29.160 --> 00:14:39.720
But because there's maybe a little bit of overlap between me pressing right and B, I am way out of sync, and I will just jump into the air and miss my set.

00:14:39.920 --> 00:14:42.380
No, I completely agree. I mean, that happened to me the entire time, too.

00:14:43.420 --> 00:14:50.720
Yeah. Yeah, I think that, if anything, like, they should have been able to have, like, a way that you can just casually play.

00:14:50.820 --> 00:14:53.380
And then, like, if you want—I mean, it's hard to do that on NES controllers.

00:14:53.540 --> 00:14:54.360
A simple mode, yeah.

00:14:54.500 --> 00:15:06.460
But, like, it's funny because, like, Mario Tennis Aces has that, you know, where it's, like, here's just a mode where you just want to play your regular tennis, and then here's another mode where, like, you can do trick shots and build up a meter and slam and serve, you know?

00:15:06.500 --> 00:15:07.700
It's, like, all that stuff.

00:15:07.740 --> 00:15:20.160
And I think, like, yeah, this game would have benefited from that, but I think, you know, the idea with these games, at least for me, these Nintendo first-party sports title games are supposed to be, like, an easy entry.

00:15:20.420 --> 00:15:24.140
Right. You know, it's supposed to be something that, like, mom and dad can play with you.

00:15:24.680 --> 00:15:26.820
And I just—I'm not getting that vibe.

00:15:27.100 --> 00:15:32.000
If only because I consider myself pretty good at video games, and I didn't win a single game.

00:15:32.080 --> 00:15:32.980
I scored some points.

00:15:33.140 --> 00:15:36.700
Yes. I did not win a single game, even if I was playing Tunisia.

00:15:36.920 --> 00:15:45.140
Yeah. And to that point, yeah, I think that, like, just picking up this game for any of us, it seems like, or for really most people that are just picking up a game, you're not going to have fun off the bat.

00:15:45.140 --> 00:15:47.260
And you're probably not going to want to continue playing it.

00:15:47.380 --> 00:15:49.500
Right. So it really doesn't benefit.

00:15:49.780 --> 00:15:50.880
Any stray thoughts?

00:15:51.540 --> 00:15:54.580
I like the country lineup.

00:15:54.680 --> 00:15:58.040
I found it to be a very interesting mishmash of—

00:15:58.040 --> 00:16:01.560
Yeah, like, are these popular countries for volleyball?

00:16:01.560 --> 00:16:09.060
I was just surprised to see a country like Tunisia next to the Soviet Union and, like, other countries.

00:16:09.060 --> 00:16:12.840
I guess I don't know what the powerhouse countries for volleyball are.

00:16:12.840 --> 00:16:14.360
I think it's, like, Brazil.

00:16:14.360 --> 00:16:15.660
Yeah, I think it's, like, Brazil.

00:16:17.080 --> 00:16:18.520
Yeah, I don't know in the 80s.

00:16:18.560 --> 00:16:21.200
That's true. But, like, Brazil is always big in volleyball.

00:16:22.520 --> 00:16:26.580
And I think Belgium, which is also in the game, I think they're a big volleyball team.

00:16:26.660 --> 00:16:29.340
But I didn't know, like, South Korea was huge on volleyball.

00:16:29.440 --> 00:16:35.580
Maybe they are. I was also interested to see that—I thought that I had already—I had accidentally pressed a button already.

00:16:36.320 --> 00:16:39.340
And that was the reason I was, like, picking who I was playing against.

00:16:39.540 --> 00:16:41.660
I thought I could pick the team I was going to play as.

00:16:41.780 --> 00:16:43.960
But, no, it's always the United States.

00:16:44.480 --> 00:16:46.000
I thought you could play anybody.

00:16:46.160 --> 00:16:47.040
Right, right, right, right.

00:16:47.100 --> 00:16:50.440
Nope. Yeah, that is actually weird that it took us this long to get to that point.

00:16:50.520 --> 00:16:54.700
But to me, it didn't matter anyway because only the changes are the colors.

00:16:54.820 --> 00:16:56.360
The color of your pants. Yeah, the color of your pants.

00:16:56.360 --> 00:16:58.700
You're still going to air hump no matter what country you're from.

00:16:59.300 --> 00:17:00.760
Which is, like, a really nice set to me.

00:17:00.820 --> 00:17:03.420
Yeah, it's a great—like, no matter what country you're from, you're going to air hump.

00:17:03.520 --> 00:17:04.360
Yeah, you're going to air hump.

00:17:04.480 --> 00:17:06.360
I'm sure the People's Republic of China loved it.

00:17:08.320 --> 00:17:09.900
I have a little straight thought.

00:17:10.060 --> 00:17:13.240
Let me hear it. So every once in a while I like to talk about the pause screens.

00:17:13.460 --> 00:17:14.960
Yes. And in this—

00:17:15.740 --> 00:17:17.840
Pause me, Joe. Did you guys pause the game at all?

00:17:18.860 --> 00:17:20.720
You know what's funny? I know.

00:17:20.840 --> 00:17:21.660
It's true, but—

00:17:21.660 --> 00:17:22.800
But did you guys pause the game?

00:17:22.800 --> 00:17:23.860
I probably did.

00:17:23.860 --> 00:17:25.060
I don't remember it, though.

00:17:25.080 --> 00:17:27.640
Well, the thing was, you can't pause during play.

00:17:27.820 --> 00:17:28.880
No, which I think is cool.

00:17:29.020 --> 00:17:31.540
It's actually kind of like you're calling a timeout.

00:17:31.620 --> 00:17:33.080
I don't know how it works in volleyball, but—

00:17:33.080 --> 00:17:35.720
That's how it's described. It's like a timeout in between plays.

00:17:36.640 --> 00:17:40.420
So—and then your whole team, like, runs over to the sidelines.

00:17:40.660 --> 00:17:41.840
Like, it doesn't actually freeze.

00:17:42.180 --> 00:17:43.620
Oh, that's a nice touch. So I was like, yeah, that is—

00:17:43.620 --> 00:17:45.720
And it's also nice that you can't— I did, so I totally never paused this game.

00:17:45.980 --> 00:17:46.920
No, I definitely didn't pause.

00:17:46.920 --> 00:17:48.680
I was like, wait, and, like, pause what's in the air and, like, decide where you're going to go.

00:17:48.680 --> 00:17:51.500
I think that's probably what I tried to do and then was like, oh, you can't pause during this game.

00:17:51.500 --> 00:17:55.200
It's broken. Like, so many other things.

00:17:55.440 --> 00:17:58.740
All right, I have one straight thought, and then we'll go into the essential games list.

00:17:59.360 --> 00:18:03.580
This comes from the game FAQ for this game.

00:18:03.800 --> 00:18:04.660
Like, the forum?

00:18:05.160 --> 00:18:07.880
No, the walkthrough guide for this game.

00:18:08.000 --> 00:18:11.460
And this kid loves volleyball, and he just kind of went—

00:18:11.460 --> 00:18:14.540
All his FAQ goes through is just, like, the strategy of it.

00:18:14.540 --> 00:18:20.100
But he writes, very interestingly, at the end of his game FAQ, he writes, in all caps,

00:18:20.520 --> 00:18:26.140
while playing this game, and then the body is, I put my hand in a ceiling fan.

00:18:26.580 --> 00:18:29.080
It was off, so I just suffered minor scratches.

00:18:31.020 --> 00:18:33.340
Like, he just needed to tell us?

00:18:33.880 --> 00:18:34.700
I love those.

00:18:34.800 --> 00:18:40.380
What year was that? Game FAQ, that was like, you know, if I had to guess, it was probably, like, early 2000s, 2002.

00:18:40.840 --> 00:18:43.760
That's when a lot of these NES guides were done, and no one's touched them since.

00:18:43.760 --> 00:18:45.080
So one quick thing.

00:18:45.320 --> 00:18:46.380
It's a very straight thought.

00:18:46.580 --> 00:18:52.560
Yeah. One thing I love about, like, the early 2000s internet, it was like, you could just mention

00:18:52.560 --> 00:18:53.720
that part of your day.

00:18:54.120 --> 00:18:55.900
Right. It didn't matter.

00:18:55.920 --> 00:18:57.840
Maybe it was a strategy we don't know about.

00:18:57.960 --> 00:19:00.560
Maybe we should try putting our hand in a ceiling fan.

00:19:00.780 --> 00:19:01.680
As long as it's off.

00:19:01.880 --> 00:19:05.980
Well, it's off. I mean, I know that, like, Twitter is just, like, built for that.

00:19:06.140 --> 00:19:10.340
Yeah, yeah. But, I mean, just to throw that into an FAQ at the end, that used to just be very endearing.

00:19:10.620 --> 00:19:12.400
Yep. Mm-hmm. Okay.

00:19:12.620 --> 00:19:17.060
So, with that in mind, let's do the Essential Games List.

00:19:19.900 --> 00:19:22.120
All right. It's the Essential Games List.

00:19:22.280 --> 00:19:26.040
And if you don't know what that is, basically, we just go around, we talk about whether we

00:19:26.040 --> 00:19:27.580
think this game is essential today.

00:19:27.800 --> 00:19:29.040
Not in 1987.

00:19:29.800 --> 00:19:31.780
Not in the history of the NES.

00:19:31.780 --> 00:19:35.300
We're talking about, in today, is this game still worth playing?

00:19:35.720 --> 00:19:39.040
Sean. It is not worth playing, Mike.

00:19:39.460 --> 00:19:41.100
Ouch. Joe?

00:19:41.700 --> 00:19:46.020
Yeah, I mean, we, I think sports games already have a little bit of an uphill battle.

00:19:46.140 --> 00:19:49.760
I think with all three of us, definitely with me, to get on my Essential Games List.

00:19:49.940 --> 00:19:55.240
And this one's already even more complicated and just not intuitive enough.

00:19:55.940 --> 00:19:59.700
Yeah. I'll also say that it's not essential, so it's off.

00:20:00.080 --> 00:20:01.140
And thank goodness.

00:20:01.400 --> 00:20:03.920
Because people would seriously doubt our judgment.

00:20:04.740 --> 00:20:09.260
Well, it really was actually the, like, he already said no, so it couldn't have been on.

00:20:09.280 --> 00:20:10.140
Oh, interesting.

00:20:10.400 --> 00:20:12.140
Okay. You don't have to sway his eyes.

00:20:12.840 --> 00:20:16.280
I'm sorry that I pretended like I had power that I don't.

00:20:16.280 --> 00:20:32.060
So, I guess, just to echo some thoughts here, I think what really bugs me about this particular game is, you know, we've seen Nintendo handle, like, 8-bit sports games in a relatively okay way.

00:20:32.060 --> 00:20:36.860
We had some beef with baseball and the fact that you can't control the outfield and that makes the game kind of wild.

00:20:36.860 --> 00:20:41.400
We had some beef with, like, you know, the way that tennis handled the Z-axis.

00:20:41.400 --> 00:20:49.980
But in this one, I have a lot of complaints, and I don't know if that's because, like, Pac-Softnica, like, got involved as, like, a second party kind of thing.

00:20:50.280 --> 00:20:55.760
But, like, that just bothered me that, like, you know, Nintendo would rush out a sports game like this.

00:20:56.000 --> 00:20:57.000
Thankfully, I did check.

00:20:57.120 --> 00:21:01.700
We're going to get some more volleyball games, and I do like volleyball, so I'd like to see a competent one.

00:21:01.820 --> 00:21:03.600
I don't know if they're going to play differently than this.

00:21:03.660 --> 00:21:05.120
They might play exactly like this.

00:21:05.120 --> 00:21:13.260
But while we're here, you know, like, this is a pretty rough entry, and I would feel burned if I spent 40 bucks on this back in the day.

00:21:13.740 --> 00:21:15.600
Oof. Definitely. All right.

00:21:15.760 --> 00:21:19.040
That is all the time we have for this episode of Nostalgia.

00:21:19.400 --> 00:21:22.560
Join us next time for Rush and Attack.

